17.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation between Choice B and Choice C remains split between asymptotic behavior and strict finite-sample definitions of bias:
- Camp B (supermajority: 12 colleagues) argues that the explicit premise "The number of samples is arbitrarily large" sets the calculation in an asymptotic regime ($n \to \infty$). In this limit, under the standard neutral coalescent, the total expected loss of segregating sites across all frequency classes is bounded by a finite constant ($\mathbb{E}[\Delta S] \le -\theta \ln(1-q) < \infty$), while the harmonic normalizer diverges logarithmically ($a_n \sim \ln n \to \infty$), causing the bias of Watterson's estimator to evaluate to 0. Similarly, the denominator discrepancy $\frac{a_n - a_{n(1-q)}}{a_n} \sim \frac{\mathcal{O}(1)}{\ln n} \to 0$. In contrast, nucleotide diversity ($\pi$) suffers from allele frequency dilution ($p \to (1-q)p$), producing an irreducible $\mathcal{O}(1)$ bias ($\approx -q\theta \neq 0$) that never attenuates with sample size. Under test-construction principles, setting $n$ arbitrarily large breaks the symmetry between the two statistics; if Choice C were intended, this condition would be superfluous since both estimators are trivially biased for finite $n$.
- Camp C (6 colleagues) argues from formal statistical terminology: an estimator where $\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[\hat{\theta}_n] = \theta$ with $\mathbb{E}[\hat{\theta}_n] < \theta$ for all finite $n$ is defined as consistent / asymptotically unbiased, but strictly biased (analogous to MLE sample variance $S_n^2$). Furthermore, they highlight that imputing missing genotypes to reference yields synthetic complete sequences ("no non-missing genotypes"), forcing tools to calculate $a_n$ rather than callable depth $a_{n(1-q)}$.
- Synthesis: The pedagogical and structural design of the prompt—pairing two contrasting summary statistics under an explicit "arbitrarily large" cohort condition with random per-sample dropout—strongly indicates that the question evaluates the decoupling of frequency-blind site discovery ($\hat{\theta}_W$, unbiased in the limit) from frequency-dependent pairwise diversity ($\pi$, permanently biased). Choice B remains the best-supported answer.
}

